"""Rationale-first CliffNotes lessons aligned to the 20-chapter STC SIE manual.
Original paraphrase: teaches relationships, distinctions, exam traps and application rather than reproducing the manual.
"""
LESSONS={
1:{"big":"Understand who is doing what, where the transaction occurs, and who handles it after execution.","why":["Broker versus dealer is a capacity question: broker = agent for someone else; dealer = principal trading from inventory. The same firm can act in either capacity on different trades.","Primary versus secondary is about where the money goes. Primary-market proceeds raise capital for the issuer; secondary-market proceeds go to the selling investor.","Third and fourth markets are easy to confuse: third = exchange-listed securities trading away from the exchange; fourth = institution-to-institution trading.","Clearing confirms and processes trade obligations; settlement is the payment-and-delivery completion. DTCC infrastructure supports clearing, settlement and custody."],"traps":["Do not assume every broker-dealer trade is agency.","ECNs match buyers and sellers electronically and act in agency capacity; dark pools conceal displayed quotes to reduce market impact for large trades.","Introducing firms can face customers while clearing firms perform back-office functions."],"apply":"When a question names a participant, first ask: issuer, investor, agent, principal, market venue, or post-trade processor?"},
2:{"big":"Regulation questions are usually jurisdiction and purpose questions: which law/regulator covers this activity?","why":["1933 Act = distribution/new issues and disclosure. 1934 Act = secondary markets, SEC framework and broker-dealer market regulation. The exam often tests the contrast rather than dates alone.","SEC is the federal regulator; FINRA regulates member broker-dealers; MSRB writes municipal securities rules; state administrators enforce Blue-Sky laws.","Investment Company Act regulates investment companies; Investment Advisers Act regulates advisers. Keep the entity being regulated straight.","SIPC addresses customer assets when a member broker-dealer fails; it is not insurance against investment loss."],"traps":["MSRB writes rules but does not function as the primary enforcement body for all municipal participants.","SEC registration does not mean the SEC approves an investment's merit.","A market decline is not an SIPC-covered loss."],"apply":"Identify the activity first—new issue, secondary trade, adviser, fund, municipal business, state registration, failed broker-dealer—then choose the governing rule or regulator."},
3:{"big":"Equity questions test ownership rights, priority, corporate capitalization and which stock feature solves an investor need.","why":["Common shareholders are residual owners: voting and upside potential come with lower liquidation priority. Preferred sacrifices much of common's upside/voting profile for dividend/liquidation preference.","Authorized is the ceiling; issued is what the company has distributed; treasury is repurchased stock; outstanding = issued minus treasury.","Cumulative preferred protects missed dividends by carrying arrears forward before common dividends resume.","ADRs provide U.S.-market access to foreign equity but still carry risks associated with the foreign issuer/country/currency."],"traps":["Treasury shares are not outstanding and do not vote or receive dividends while held by issuer.","Preferred stock is still equity, not corporate debt.","Limited liability does not guarantee the stock cannot fall to zero."],"apply":"For stock questions, classify the investor's objective: control/voting, income priority, growth, foreign exposure, or ownership percentage protection."},
4:{"big":"Debt is a creditor relationship. Most questions reduce to cash flows, priority, interest-rate sensitivity, credit risk or an embedded feature.","why":["Coupon determines stated interest; market price determines current yield; YTM incorporates coupon plus the premium/discount effect through maturity.","Rates and existing bond prices move inversely because old fixed coupons become more or less attractive versus newly issued debt.","Longer maturities generally have more interest-rate exposure because the investor is locked into the fixed cash flow longer.","A call benefits the issuer when refinancing becomes attractive; a put benefits the investor by creating an exit right."],"traps":["Coupon does not change merely because market price changes.","A premium bond can still pay the same dollar coupon as when issued; the lower yield comes from paying more for those cash flows.","Conversion is optional for the holder; it is not the same as maturity repayment."],"apply":"When rates change, immediately think opposite bond-price direction; then ask whether maturity, callability or credit quality amplifies the risk."},
5:{"big":"Learn debt by source of repayment and risk, not just names.","why":["Treasuries are direct federal obligations; T-bills are short-term discount instruments while notes/bonds pay stated interest over longer terms.","GNMA mortgage-backed securities have explicit federal backing; FNMA/FHLMC are GSE-related, not identical direct Treasury obligations.","GO municipal debt relies primarily on taxing power; revenue debt relies primarily on project/facility revenues.","Corporate secured debt has pledged collateral; a debenture relies on general credit. Commercial paper is short-term unsecured corporate borrowing.","Mortgage-backed investors face prepayment risk when falling rates encourage homeowners to refinance and return principal early."],"traps":["Do not choose GO merely because a municipality issued the bond—look at repayment source.","Government-sponsored does not automatically mean direct U.S. government guarantee.","Falling rates can help ordinary bond prices but create reinvestment/prepayment problems for mortgage-backed investors."],"apply":"Underline who promises repayment, what backs it, maturity, and whether cash flows can return earlier than expected."},
6:{"big":"Returns questions test what the investor actually earns and how price, income, taxes and time affect that return.","why":["Current yield = annual interest/current market price. It ignores the maturity gain/loss, which is why YTM is more comprehensive.","Discount bond: moving from purchase price up to par adds return, so YTM exceeds current yield and coupon. Premium bond: moving down to par subtracts return, so YTM is below current yield and coupon.","Total return combines income with appreciation/depreciation relative to investment.","Ex-date determines whether a purchaser receives an upcoming distribution; record date identifies holders on issuer records."],"traps":["The manual explicitly says exact YTM calculation is not required; understand what goes into it and the yield relationships instead.","Basis can mean YTM in bond quotation context; one basis point is 0.01%.","A stock dividend changes share count/cost allocation; it is not the same as receiving a cash dividend."],"apply":"Write the cash flows: what did investor pay, what income arrives, what value is received at sale/maturity? That reveals the correct return concept."},
7:{"big":"Packaged-product questions test structure, pricing, fees, liquidity and sales-practice differences.","why":["Open-end mutual funds transact with the fund at next calculated NAV (plus applicable sales charge); closed-end funds trade between investors intraday at market prices that can be above or below NAV.","UIT portfolios are generally fixed rather than actively managed day to day.","Class A economics emphasize front-end load and breakpoint mechanics; Class C generally shifts more cost to ongoing expenses/possible short CDSC.","Breakpoints, rights of accumulation and letters of intent exist to reduce eligible sales charges as investment size rises."],"traps":["Mutual fund shares do not trade intraday like common stock/ETFs.","A breakpoint sale is a sales-practice violation, not a customer benefit.","Dollar-cost averaging cannot guarantee profit or prevent loss."],"apply":"Ask whether the product is redeemable with the issuer or exchange-traded, then compare pricing, sales charges and management style."},
8:{"big":"Annuity questions revolve around who bears investment risk, which account holds assets, tax treatment and contract roles.","why":["Fixed annuity guarantees are supported by insurer's general account, so insurer bears investment risk; variable contract values fluctuate with separate-account performance, so owner bears investment risk.","Accumulation phase builds value; annuity phase converts accumulated value into an income stream.","Owner controls the contract; annuitant's life is used for benefit calculations; beneficiary receives applicable death benefits.","Tax-deferred means tax is postponed, not eliminated. Qualified versus nonqualified funding changes basis and distribution taxation."],"traps":["Separate account is not the same as insurer general account.","1035 exchange can defer tax when requirements are met; it does not make every replacement suitable.","529 and ABLE accounts are municipal fund securities with specific qualified-use rules."],"apply":"For any annuity scenario identify four things: fixed/variable, general/separate account, accumulation/annuity phase, and owner/annuitant/beneficiary."},
9:{"big":"Alternative investments are distinguished by legal structure, liquidity, tax treatment, leverage and unique risk.","why":["ETF is an exchange-traded pooled security; ETN is unsecured issuer debt linked to a benchmark—so ETN adds issuer credit risk.","Leveraged/inverse ETFs target DAILY multiples or inverse returns; compounding means long-period results can diverge sharply from simple multiples.","DPP/limited partnership structures may pass tax items through; general partner manages and bears broader liability while limited partners remain passive to preserve limited liability.","REITs provide real-estate exposure but equity REITs own properties while mortgage REITs emphasize real-estate financing."],"traps":["ETN is not a fund merely because its return tracks an index.","Daily leveraged ETF objective is not a promise of the same multiple over months or years.","Alternative products can be illiquid and are not automatically suitable because of tax features."],"apply":"Classify the wrapper first—fund, unsecured note, partnership, REIT, hedge fund—then attach the wrapper-specific risk."},
10:{"big":"Options become manageable when you separate RIGHT from OBLIGATION and BULLISH from BEARISH.","why":["Buyer pays premium for a right; writer receives premium and accepts an obligation. Call = buy underlying; put = sell underlying.","Long call and short put are bullish; long put and short call are bearish.","Intrinsic value is only the in-the-money amount; remaining premium is time value.","Hedging uses options as insurance around an existing exposure: protective put protects long stock; covered call generates premium but caps upside."],"traps":["Buyer max loss is premium, but writer risk can be much larger; uncovered call loss is theoretically unlimited.","In-the-money does not automatically mean the position is profitable because premium affects breakeven.","Exercise, liquidation and expiration are different ways an option position can end."],"apply":"Draw four boxes: call/put × buy/sell. Put RIGHT or OBLIGATION, bullish/bearish, max loss and breakeven in each."},
11:{"big":"Offering questions follow a timeline: structure the underwriting, register/disclose, cooling-off, effective date, then sell.","why":["Firm commitment shifts resale risk to underwriter; best efforts leaves more sale risk with issuer because underwriter acts as agent.","During cooling-off, a preliminary prospectus and nonbinding indications of interest are permitted, but final sales/payment are not.","The SEC reviews disclosure completeness; effectiveness is not an endorsement of investment quality.","Private/exempt offerings use different exemptions and investor qualifications; Rule 144A focuses on resales to QIBs."],"traps":["Selling group helps distribute but does not have the same underwriting liability as syndicate members.","Red herring omits final offering price and is not the final statutory prospectus.","An indication of interest during cooling-off is nonbinding."],"apply":"Place the scenario on the offering timeline. Most wrong answers describe an action allowed at a different stage."},
12:{"big":"Order questions ask what the customer prioritizes: execution, price protection, loss protection or entry trigger.","why":["Market order prioritizes execution, not exact price. Limit order prioritizes price but may never execute.","Stop order becomes a market order when triggered; stop-limit becomes a limit order, adding price control but increasing nonexecution risk.","Sell stops are below market and commonly protect long positions; buy stops are above market and commonly protect shorts.","Broker/agent compensation and dealer/principal compensation differ because capacity differs."],"traps":["5% policy is a fairness guideline, not an automatic maximum.","A stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed execution price.","Time/price discretion on a specific order is not the same as broad discretionary authority over security/action/amount."],"apply":"Translate the customer's sentence: 'get me filled' = market; 'not worse than this price' = limit; 'protect me after price crosses this trigger' = stop logic."},
13:{"big":"Settlement and corporate actions test who owns the economic benefit and how share/price mechanics change.","why":["Regular-way equity settlement is T+1. Settlement is when payment/delivery obligations complete.","Forward split raises share count and proportionally lowers price; reverse split does the opposite. Initial total market value is unchanged.","Ex-date and record-date mechanics determine entitlement to distributions; due bills can transfer a distribution to the economically entitled buyer.","Street-name registration makes the broker-dealer record holder while customer remains beneficial owner."],"traps":["A stock split does not create instant wealth.","Record date is not the same as ex-date.","Book-entry ownership does not mean customer lacks ownership rights."],"apply":"For corporate actions calculate shares × price before and after, then ask who is the beneficial owner on the relevant entitlement date."},
14:{"big":"Account questions test ownership, authority, credit, tax wrapper and who may act.","why":["Cash account requires full payment; margin account uses broker-dealer credit and brings Regulation T/maintenance considerations.","Full discretion requires authorization/approval; limited time-and-price discretion over an existing order is different.","JTWROS transfers a deceased owner's interest to survivors; tenants in common allows the deceased owner's share to pass through the estate.","UGMA/UTMA gift is irrevocable and custodian manages assets for the minor.","Traditional and Roth IRAs differ mainly in contribution tax treatment, distribution taxation and RMD treatment."],"traps":["A trusted contact is not automatically authorized to trade.","Custodian owns control duties but the custodial property belongs beneficially to the minor.","Joint account title changes what happens at death."],"apply":"Identify owner, authorized actor, source of buying power and tax/legal wrapper before answering."},
15:{"big":"Compliance questions are process questions: know the customer, verify identity, identify suspicious behavior, protect data, classify communications and escalate appropriately.","why":["KYC establishes essential customer facts; Reg BI governs recommendations to retail customers and requires their best interest without putting firm interests first.","AML progression: placement introduces illicit funds, layering obscures the trail, integration returns funds appearing legitimate.","CIP verifies identity; CTR addresses qualifying large cash transactions; SAR addresses qualifying suspicious activity.","Communication classification depends heavily on audience: correspondence, retail communication or institutional communication."],"traps":["CTR and SAR are not interchangeable just because a transaction involves cash.","KYC and Reg BI overlap in customer protection but answer different regulatory questions.","Privacy rules do not eliminate lawful regulatory reporting obligations."],"apply":"Ask what triggered the firm's duty: identity, recommendation, cash threshold, suspicious behavior, privacy, communication audience, or business disruption."},
16:{"big":"Prohibited-practice questions are easiest when you identify who benefits improperly and what duty is being violated.","why":["Churning = excessive activity for compensation; reverse churning = unjustified ongoing fee for inactive account.","Front-running = trading ahead of customer flow; insider trading centers on material nonpublic information and applicable duty.","Marking the close manipulates closing price; interpositioning adds an unnecessary party that disadvantages customer.","Best execution requires reasonable diligence for favorable terms; guarantees against customer securities losses are prohibited."],"traps":["High trading volume alone is not the full churning analysis; look for excessiveness relative to account and compensation motive.","Material information must matter to a reasonable investor; nonpublic means not broadly disseminated.","Borrowing/lending and sharing arrangements are restricted rather than universally allowed by customer consent."],"apply":"Name the misconduct before reading choices. If you can say 'trading ahead,' 'excessive trading,' 'MNPI,' or 'manipulating close,' distractors become easier to separate."},
17:{"big":"Registration questions test what an individual is allowed to do, what must be disclosed, and who supervises them.","why":["Passing SIE demonstrates core knowledge but does not by itself authorize registered securities activities; appropriate qualification/association is still required.","Form U4 begins individual registration and feeds CRD, which contains registration/employment/disciplinary information.","Representatives perform registered activities; principals supervise. Unregistered staff are limited to functions that do not require registration.","Continuing education includes Regulatory Element and Firm Element; MQP can preserve eligible qualifications under conditions."],"traps":["SIE is not a standalone securities license.","Blue-Sky registration is state-level and distinct from FINRA registration.","If an exam scenario asks how an RR should clarify an uncertain rule, the manual notes contacting the designated supervisor may be appropriate."],"apply":"Ask: does this activity require registration, what form/database records it, and which supervisor has responsibility?"},
18:{"big":"Employee-conduct questions test disclosure, outside activity, conflicts, gifts and what happens when association ends.","why":["U5 reports termination of registration association; U4 is the continuing registration/disclosure form; U6 is regulator-filed for specified disciplinary events.","Outside business activity and private securities transactions are not the same. Private securities transactions involve securities outside regular employment and can become selling away when required approval is absent.","Written customer complaints create recordkeeping and potentially reporting duties.","Gift rules and business entertainment differ; the context, attendance and business relationship matter."],"traps":["Calling an activity 'outside work' does not tell you whether it is an OBA or private securities transaction—look for a securities transaction.","Selling away can occur even when the representative believes the investment is good.","Termination does not erase disclosure history; BrokerCheck/CRD can preserve reportable information."],"apply":"For conduct scenarios ask: securities transaction? compensation? prior notice/approval? customer complaint? reportable event? termination?"},
19:{"big":"Economics questions connect economic conditions to rates, securities and corporate financial statements.","why":["Inflation reduces purchasing power; real interest rate approximates nominal rate minus inflation.","Business cycle moves expansion → peak → contraction → trough; cyclical businesses are more sensitive to these changes.","Fed tools affect money/credit and therefore rates. Yield-curve shape reflects rate expectations; inversion is commonly associated with recession concerns.","Balance sheet is a point-in-time financial position; income statement measures performance over a period.","Market cap = outstanding shares × price. P/E compares price with EPS; operating margin and bond coverage use income-statement data."],"traps":["GDP is domestic production; do not confuse it with nationality-based GNP.","Outstanding shares exclude treasury shares.","Depreciation reduces accounting income but is a noncash expense."],"apply":"Translate macro cause → rate/credit effect → likely security/business impact; for statements, identify whether the question asks a snapshot or a period of performance."},
20:{"big":"Risk questions test whether risk is market-wide or security-specific and which tool/product is exposed to it.","why":["Systematic risk cannot be diversified away; unsystematic risk can be reduced by diversification.","Interest-rate, market and inflation risks are broad exposures; business, credit, liquidity and issuer-specific regulatory risks are more security-specific.","Duration measures bond sensitivity to interest-rate changes; beta measures market-relative volatility/systematic exposure.","Strategic allocation is long-term target positioning; tactical allocation makes shorter-term deviations. Sector rotation is active; indexing/buy-and-hold/rebalancing are passive approaches.","Hedging uses offsetting positions to reduce a defined risk; it generally exchanges some cost/upside for protection."],"traps":["Diversification reduces company-specific risk but cannot eliminate broad market risk.","Credit risk and interest-rate risk can coexist in the same bond.","Currency and political risks matter even when the underlying company itself performs well."],"apply":"For every scenario label the source of loss first: whole market, rates, inflation, issuer, liquidity, currency, politics, credit or early repayment. Then choose the matching risk."}
}
